00:05:39.269 --> 00:05:44.789
So let's kick things off by understanding
why API security is such an important

00:05:44.789 --> 00:05:48.119
topic and why it's so relevant to PCI.

00:05:49.119 --> 00:05:54.169
And there are three important
dynamics happening now with APIs.

00:05:54.859 --> 00:05:58.034
The first is that APIs are exploding.

00:05:58.034 --> 00:06:01.404
We've known this for years
and it just continues.

00:06:01.784 --> 00:06:07.204
You've probably seen the statistic
that over 83% of all internet

00:06:07.204 --> 00:06:09.504
traffic now is API based.

00:06:09.714 --> 00:06:13.374
This is a study from Akamai, they
analyzed all their network traffic

00:06:13.384 --> 00:06:18.529
and discovered this incredible
prevalence of API-sourced traffic.

00:06:19.109 --> 00:06:23.679
Look at the API calls, a hundred
billion open banking API calls this

00:06:23.679 --> 00:06:30.049
year and expected to grow by 5X over
the next five years to 500 billion.

00:06:30.549 --> 00:06:34.604
There's really no end in sight of
the adoption of APIs, and that's

00:06:34.624 --> 00:06:38.964
thanks to just the sheer value and
benefit that these APIs provide.

00:06:39.654 --> 00:06:41.154
And they're powering virtually everything.

00:06:41.204 --> 00:06:45.734
Every e-commerce transaction, our
medical devices, our connected

00:06:45.734 --> 00:06:50.280
cars, you can lock and unlock,
start and stop engines through APIs.

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:52.720
They're really powering
virtually everything.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:56.940
But APIs are also under heavy attack.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:59.455
The stats are really quite staggering.

00:06:59.985 --> 00:07:05.515
40 to 75 billion dollars a year
in cyber loss related to APIs.

00:07:05.945 --> 00:07:12.905
For years, Gartner, the analyst firm, has
been predicting that by 2022 APIs would

00:07:12.905 --> 00:07:15.465
become "the most frequent attack vector."

00:07:15.485 --> 00:07:19.265
I think that has been borne
out and continues to be true.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:23.610
I have another course, API Security
Fundamentals, if you want to learn

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:27.540
more about specific breaches, we
go into a lot of detail there and

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:30.000
explore how APIs were exploited.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.820
So I recommend you check out
that course if you're interested.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:35.570
But the third dynamic is API security.

00:07:36.075 --> 00:07:39.105
Our APIs are seriously under-secured.

00:07:39.125 --> 00:07:45.165
And if you just look at testing alone,
this report from stateofapis.com looks at

00:07:45.175 --> 00:07:51.575
all of the testing we do on APIs, and only
4% of that effort is focused on security.

00:07:52.305 --> 00:07:53.615
And there's a reason for this.

00:07:54.165 --> 00:07:56.955
How we build applications has
fundamentally changed, but

00:07:56.955 --> 00:07:58.485
security is still keeping up.

00:08:00.749 --> 00:08:04.449
I want to share some thoughts from a
recent Gartner report that really nailed

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:09.359
the challenges and the changes happening
in application development and security.

00:08:09.839 --> 00:08:13.879
This comes from a Hype Cycle
report on application security that

00:08:13.879 --> 00:08:16.299
Gartner published in July 2023.

00:08:16.769 --> 00:08:21.629
Some of the key trends are that we're
now developing apps more quickly, in more

00:08:21.639 --> 00:08:27.089
agile ways, we're relying more and more on
developers to secure the code to integrate

00:08:27.089 --> 00:08:29.129
security into their applications.

00:08:29.999 --> 00:08:33.499
And we've changed how we deploy
and operate our applications with

00:08:33.749 --> 00:08:35.619
things like cloud native strategies.

00:08:35.649 --> 00:08:38.529
And these are challenging our
traditional and established

00:08:38.529 --> 00:08:44.123
approaches  to application development
and provisioning and deployment.

00:08:45.373 --> 00:08:50.193
However, our existing application
security controls have not kept up,

00:08:50.363 --> 00:08:52.283
and they've become less effective.

00:08:54.113 --> 00:08:55.753
Dionisio from Gartner summarizes it well.

00:08:56.513 --> 00:09:02.013
API attacks have resulted in a stream
of data breaches, and DevSecOps teams

00:09:02.053 --> 00:09:06.623
have increasingly become interested
in API testing and security.

00:09:06.973 --> 00:09:12.033
However, our traditional AST tools,
application security testing tools,

00:09:12.043 --> 00:09:15.473
were not originally designed to
test for some of the unique types

00:09:15.473 --> 00:09:20.273
of vulnerabilities associated
with typical attacks against APIs.

00:09:20.303 --> 00:09:22.563
And his point is really spot on.

00:09:22.643 --> 00:09:26.753
API attacks are fundamentally
different and let's examine why.

00:09:28.396 --> 00:09:33.736
So organizations have long used tools
like SAST and DAST, static and dynamic

00:09:33.736 --> 00:09:36.206
application security testing solutions.

00:09:36.706 --> 00:09:41.206
And these are very mature, well known
tools that look for poor coding practices,

00:09:41.236 --> 00:09:47.856
injection flaws, cross site scripting,
things like CSRF, cross site request

00:09:47.856 --> 00:09:52.651
forgery, SSRF, server side request
forgery, misconfigurations and the like.

00:09:53.561 --> 00:09:57.891
These tools are looking for common
vulnerabilities, common flaws that

00:09:57.921 --> 00:09:59.801
might exist in your applications.

00:10:00.671 --> 00:10:06.311
But if you look at the breaches
that successfully exploit APIs and

00:10:06.311 --> 00:10:10.470
result in the losses of hundreds
of millions of records, this is

00:10:10.471 --> 00:10:12.421
not what's causing those breaches.

00:10:12.431 --> 00:10:13.551
It's not injections.

00:10:13.551 --> 00:10:15.061
It's not cross site scripting.

00:10:15.431 --> 00:10:18.221
API breaches exploit something different.

00:10:18.541 --> 00:10:23.771
Typically business logic flaws
or gaps in authorization or weak

00:10:23.771 --> 00:10:25.701
authentication and the like.

00:10:26.221 --> 00:10:30.241
And if you want to keep up on this
there's a great site, apisecurity.io,

00:10:30.261 --> 00:10:34.061
where they capture these types of
events and exploits and breaches.

00:10:34.501 --> 00:10:38.421
And you'll see how API breaches
happen just doesn't look like our

00:10:38.691 --> 00:10:40.791
traditional web application attacks.

00:10:41.411 --> 00:10:44.201
And this graphic down
below reinforces the point.

00:10:44.771 --> 00:10:49.051
Our web scanners, for the most
part, are scanning at the UI layer.

00:10:49.471 --> 00:10:51.311
Mobile UI, Web UI.

00:10:51.791 --> 00:10:54.061
And there are two issues
with this approach.

00:10:54.361 --> 00:11:00.334
First, we've been relying on the UI layer
for too long to do more than presentation.

00:11:00.914 --> 00:11:03.344
The UI layer is also providing security.

00:11:03.354 --> 00:11:07.234
It's filtering what data users
can see, what actions they can

00:11:07.234 --> 00:11:09.274
take, what buttons they can press.

00:11:09.984 --> 00:11:11.254
And this is a problem.

00:11:11.554 --> 00:11:15.684
The UI should really be about
presentation, not about security.

00:11:16.824 --> 00:11:21.414
And the reason why is that
UIs are powered by APIs.

00:11:21.994 --> 00:11:26.024
And attackers are going to go right
around your UI straight to the API.

00:11:26.674 --> 00:11:31.854
And at the API level, you don't have
the benefit of this UI and the filtering

00:11:31.854 --> 00:11:33.634
and the controls that it's providing.

00:11:34.084 --> 00:11:38.804
So if an attacker can dream up a
request for an API, they can send it.

00:11:39.424 --> 00:11:42.394
And if your backend app logic
doesn't know to block those

00:11:42.394 --> 00:11:44.214
requests, they'll get executed.

00:11:44.434 --> 00:11:46.934
And that's how API breaches happen.

00:11:47.674 --> 00:11:51.394
Again, we have a whole course
focused on this topic, the API

00:11:51.394 --> 00:11:54.914
Security Fundamentals course, where
you can learn a lot more on this.

00:11:56.980 --> 00:11:59.960
No API security course would be
complete without a mention of

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.170
the OWASP API Security Top 10.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:07.110
The OWASP is the Open Web
Application Security Project.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:11.650
It's a community that has produced truly
foundational application security guides

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:14.540
and is famous for its top 10 lists.

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:21.220
In 2019, OWASP added a dedicated top
10 for API security, which differed

00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:23.300
quite a bit from their web app top 10.

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:27.640
These lists are widely considered
to be industry standards for

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:29.750
how to secure your applications.

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:35.410
And in 2023, The OWASP community
released an updated version of the

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:37.430
API top 10, which you can see here.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:40.730
There've been some changes,
some additions and deletions,

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:42.510
and some items remain the same.

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:45.600
The top two are still at the top.

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:49.780
And for good reason, the vast
majority of API breaches violate

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:51.510
one or both of these items.

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:56.200
Number one is Broken Object Level
Authorization, also known as BOLA.

00:12:56.985 --> 00:13:02.035
And it refers to the ability of,
say, one user being able to access

00:13:02.075 --> 00:13:03.965
another user's data through an API.

00:13:04.785 --> 00:13:09.265
And there are many examples of these which
we cover in some of our other courses.

00:13:10.105 --> 00:13:14.865
Number two is Broken Authentication, which
is also a frequent cause of breaches.

00:13:15.205 --> 00:13:20.765
Many times, API endpoints are
left open with no authentication,

00:13:20.795 --> 00:13:23.965
with organizations assuming these
endpoints won't be discovered.

00:13:24.435 --> 00:13:28.965
You should always assume they will
be discovered, as attackers will try

00:13:28.965 --> 00:13:31.325
their best to find these endpoints.

00:13:31.365 --> 00:13:34.215
And you should protect all
endpoints with security, regardless

00:13:34.235 --> 00:13:38.185
of whether they are documented,
published, internal, or external.

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:43.390
One of the new entries on the OWASP
top 10 is number six, Unrestricted

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.250
Access to Sensitive Business Flows.

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:50.320
And this is the first time that OWASP has
referenced business logic specifically,

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:55.090
recognizing the opportunities
to exploit logic flaws in your

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:57.530
applications to access sensitive data.

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:04.360
And you'll see that this concept of
business logic appears in DSS 4.0 as well.

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:07.940
There's a specific requirement that
we'll cover to test business logic

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:11.970
of your applications and your APIs
in order to find any vulnerabilities.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:17.410
We've got a whole course dedicated
just to the OWASP API Security Top 10,

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:19.910
which you can also find on our website.

00:14:24.552 --> 00:14:28.712
If you're new to PCI, let's have a very
quick review about what it's all about.

00:14:29.522 --> 00:14:35.092
PCI stands for Payment Card Industry,
and the primary goal is to safeguard

00:14:35.212 --> 00:14:39.112
sensitive cardholder data and maintain
trust in the payment card industry.

00:14:40.172 --> 00:14:45.322
Until PCI was created, the five major
credit card brands, Visa, MasterCard,

00:14:45.402 --> 00:14:50.052
American Express, Discover, JCB all
had their own security standards.

00:14:50.122 --> 00:14:55.952
And in 2004, they all got together,
formed the PCI, and created a unified

00:14:55.952 --> 00:15:00.452
set of security requirements, starting
with defining what data needs to be

00:15:00.452 --> 00:15:02.332
protected, which you can see below.

00:15:03.022 --> 00:15:07.152
Data like the primary account
number or PAN, cardholder name,

00:15:07.172 --> 00:15:12.487
expiration, verification, stripe
and chip data, PINs and more.

00:15:13.247 --> 00:15:15.337
Now PCI is not a law.

00:15:15.427 --> 00:15:18.097
There's no government
enforced regulation here.

00:15:18.137 --> 00:15:22.797
But it is a contractual obligation
that applies to any entity that

00:15:22.797 --> 00:15:27.157
stores, transmits, or processes
credit card transactions.

00:15:27.913 --> 00:15:30.153
You can see the timeline
of PCI here on the right.

00:15:30.183 --> 00:15:31.843
It was launched in 2004.

00:15:32.363 --> 00:15:40.333
In 2006 and 2009 we saw major credit card
breaches at TJ Maxx, where 45 million

00:15:40.663 --> 00:15:45.236
records were breached, and at Heartland
Payment Systems, where 130million records

00:15:45.236 --> 00:15:49.545
were breached, which really reinforced
the criticality of PCI and led to the

00:15:49.545 --> 00:15:59.820
release of DSS 2.0 in 2010, 3.0 in 2013
and 4.0 another nine years later in 2022.

00:16:02.029 --> 00:16:05.599
Within PCI, there are a number of
different security standards, and

00:16:05.599 --> 00:16:07.179
you can see some of them here below.

00:16:07.839 --> 00:16:12.209
Each focuses on a different aspect
of payment card transactions.

00:16:12.429 --> 00:16:16.749
For example, there are standards
on encryption, on PIN security,

00:16:16.779 --> 00:16:18.359
on point of sale devices.

00:16:19.129 --> 00:16:22.758
The one standard that affects virtually
every organization that's covered by

00:16:22.768 --> 00:16:27.898
PCI, organizations that store, process,
or transmit credit card transactions

00:16:27.918 --> 00:16:30.708
is DSS, the Data Security Standard.

00:16:31.118 --> 00:16:33.858
And that's what we'll be focusing
on for the rest of this course.

00:16:38.122 --> 00:16:40.812
So let's start at the top with
the data security standard.

00:16:41.312 --> 00:16:43.562
Throughout this course, I
will use direct excerpts from

00:16:43.562 --> 00:16:45.502
the DSS documentation itself.

00:16:46.002 --> 00:16:49.242
You can find the documentation
and much more in the PCI Document

00:16:49.242 --> 00:16:52.252
Library at PCISecurityStandards.org.

00:16:53.392 --> 00:16:56.472
Let's focus first on the
six primary goals of DSS.

00:16:57.232 --> 00:16:59.102
Running secure networks and systems.

00:16:59.382 --> 00:17:00.672
Protecting account data.

00:17:01.252 --> 00:17:02.912
Finding and fixing vulnerabilities.

00:17:03.202 --> 00:17:04.492
Control who can access what.

00:17:05.222 --> 00:17:06.272
Testing and monitoring.

00:17:06.712 --> 00:17:08.667
And maintaining a security policy.

00:17:09.416 --> 00:17:13.729
Those six goals are further broken
down into 12 DSS Requirement

00:17:13.729 --> 00:17:15.309
categories, which you can see here.

00:17:15.929 --> 00:17:20.419
Everything from updating default passwords
to controlling user access to testing

00:17:20.429 --> 00:17:22.219
apps for vulnerabilities is covered here.

00:17:22.979 --> 00:17:27.289
Each requirement provides a detailed
description, how to test for compliance,

00:17:27.289 --> 00:17:29.789
and guidance on Purpose and Best Practice.

00:17:30.589 --> 00:17:33.784
We are going to focus on
Requirement 6, Develop and Maintain

00:17:33.784 --> 00:17:35.284
Secure Systems and Software.

00:17:35.674 --> 00:17:39.734
Here is where the DSS specifies how
organizations must develop, test,

00:17:39.764 --> 00:17:41.504
and operate secure applications.

00:17:46.545 --> 00:17:49.055
Next, let's dig into what's new in 4.0.

00:17:49.905 --> 00:17:54.030
DSS 4.0 is a huge update to
the Data Security Standard.

00:17:54.120 --> 00:18:01.750
As you can see, the requirements doc
itself grew by over 2.5X to 360 pages.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:06.340
The PCI Security Standards Council
has outlined four primary goals

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:11.390
for the new 4.0 standard, starting
with, of course continuing to meet

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:15.310
the security standards and security
needs of the payment industry.

00:18:16.030 --> 00:18:20.100
Promoting the concept of security
being a continuous process, not a

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:22.750
one time or periodic assessment.

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:27.800
Adding flexibility for different
methodologies and recognizing that not

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:29.930
all organizations do things the same way.

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:34.850
So DSS 4.0 provides some options for
compliance and certain requirements.

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:38.140
And finally to enhance validation methods.

00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:42.650
Being more explicit about what compliance
looks like and how to assess it.

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:48.280
Now what's especially noteworthy
with respect to APIs is that in

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:50.650
3.0 there was no mention of APIs.

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:58.330
And APIs appear in multiple sections
throughout Requirement 6 of the DSS 4.0.

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:03.570
And even where APIs aren't explicitly
mentioned they still are very relevant.

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:08.450
And one way to think about this is
if your APIs are in scope for your

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:12.930
attackers, then they ought to be in
scope for your security programs too.

00:19:13.930 --> 00:19:17.980
Let's look at what the compliance
timeline looks like for 4.0 real quick.

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:26.174
The 4.0 standard was published
in Q1 and Q2 of 2022 and as of Q2

00:19:26.174 --> 00:19:31.984
2022, organizations could begin
certifying against the 4.0 standard.

00:19:32.664 --> 00:19:36.284
That's the beginning of the transition
period that you see here in the timeline.

00:19:36.814 --> 00:19:41.964
The deadline for 4.0
compliance is March 31, 2024.

00:19:42.314 --> 00:19:46.964
Meaning all organizations
must comply with the DSS 4.0

00:19:46.964 --> 00:19:49.784
requirements by the end of Q1 ' 24.

00:19:51.344 --> 00:19:57.034
There are a few requirements that the DSS
has provided a longer compliance period.

00:19:57.954 --> 00:20:02.234
Those are all required by March 31, 2025.

00:20:02.252 --> 00:20:04.922
I'll flag those where
relevant in the coming slides.

00:20:05.936 --> 00:20:09.616
And if you want to learn more about any
of this, here are a few useful resources.

00:20:10.006 --> 00:20:12.376
I've shared the links
in the course materials.

00:20:16.089 --> 00:20:20.049
This brings us to the core of this course,
reviewing the specific requirements in

00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:23.099
DSS 4.0 and what they mean for APIs.

00:20:23.619 --> 00:20:26.568
We'll be reviewing about a dozen
sections in the Data Security

00:20:26.568 --> 00:20:31.018
Standard, mostly from Requirement
6, and examining in detail what is

00:20:31.018 --> 00:20:32.678
being required and how to achieve it.

00:20:33.398 --> 00:20:37.218
In each case, I'll show you the actual
text from the DSS document itself,

00:20:37.718 --> 00:20:41.908
discuss how this requirement applies
to APIs, and provide best practices

00:20:41.908 --> 00:20:44.438
to comply and keep your APIs secure.

00:20:45.038 --> 00:20:45.938
So let's get started.

00:20:50.155 --> 00:20:56.205
Starting with 2.2.7, which states " all
non console administrative access is

00:20:56.215 --> 00:20:58.217
encrypted using strong  cryptography."

00:20:58.957 --> 00:21:02.747
I bring this one up as this
requirement explicitly mentions APIs.

00:21:02.857 --> 00:21:05.577
In fact, it's the first time
the Data Security Standard

00:21:05.577 --> 00:21:07.547
has flagged API specifically.

00:21:08.197 --> 00:21:12.097
In this case, the requirement highlights
that encrypted admin console access

00:21:12.127 --> 00:21:17.007
is required, not just for browser
based UIs, but also for application

00:21:17.007 --> 00:21:19.107
programming interfaces or APIs.

00:21:19.622 --> 00:21:22.652
So it's a pretty straightforward
requirement, and of course, all our

00:21:22.652 --> 00:21:25.852
application communication should
be done over encrypted channels.

00:21:30.029 --> 00:21:34.959
Now we'll get into the core of DSS 4.0,
which is Requirement 6, "Developing and

00:21:34.959 --> 00:21:37.099
Maintaining Secure Systems and Software."

00:21:37.949 --> 00:21:42.319
Here you see a number of sub requirements,
which we'll get into, but one term

00:21:42.319 --> 00:21:46.199
you'll see throughout Requirement 6
is " Bespoke and Custom Software."

00:21:46.869 --> 00:21:51.509
This refers to any custom code developed
by your organization or provided to you

00:21:51.509 --> 00:21:56.627
by another party, and this is subject
to these DSS requirements, specifically

00:21:56.637 --> 00:22:01.382
if that software stores, processes,
or transmits account data, or it could

00:22:01.382 --> 00:22:03.162
impact the security of account data.

00:22:04.192 --> 00:22:06.302
This definition encompasses APIs.

00:22:06.682 --> 00:22:11.642
So if your bespoke and custom software
incorporates APIs, those are in scope.

00:22:12.502 --> 00:22:16.262
So as we review the requirements
throughout DSS 4.0, if it applies to

00:22:16.262 --> 00:22:17.882
your custom developed applications.

00:22:18.197 --> 00:22:20.007
it applies to those APIs as well.

00:22:20.737 --> 00:22:25.467
Now, here at the bottom, PCI has provided
guidance that for custom software,

00:22:25.817 --> 00:22:29.507
"numerous vulnerabilities can be
avoided by applying software lifecycle

00:22:29.517 --> 00:22:31.747
processes and secure coding techniques."

00:22:32.507 --> 00:22:36.717
Organizations should strive to create a
culture of security across the application

00:22:36.717 --> 00:22:41.111
development team, understanding
application risks, writing more secure

00:22:41.131 --> 00:22:44.321
code, testing for vulnerabilities,
and fixing ahead of production.

00:22:47.831 --> 00:22:53.531
Moving ahead to section 6.2.1, " Bespoke
and Custom Software are developed securely

00:22:53.531 --> 00:22:58.621
as follows: based on industry standards
or best practices for secure development,

00:22:59.611 --> 00:23:05.761
in accordance with the PCI DSS, and
incorporating consideration of information

00:23:05.761 --> 00:23:09.361
security issues during each stage of
the software development lifecycle."

00:23:10.401 --> 00:23:11.931
That's the requirement of 6.2.1.

00:23:12.051 --> 00:23:17.606
Further, The DSS standard instructs
organizations to "understand how sensitive

00:23:17.606 --> 00:23:21.536
data is handled by the application
to inform how to protect the data."

00:23:22.286 --> 00:23:25.196
The implication for APIs
here is pretty clear.

00:23:25.396 --> 00:23:29.396
PCI wants organizations to bake-in
security as early as possible in the

00:23:29.396 --> 00:23:33.816
creation of custom code, or "shift
left" security as you may have heard it.

00:23:34.641 --> 00:23:38.671
Stating that "DSS requirements
must be considered when developing

00:23:38.671 --> 00:23:42.541
software by design rather than trying
to retrofit the software later."

00:23:43.381 --> 00:23:46.631
To achieve this requirement,
organizations need to ensure application

00:23:46.631 --> 00:23:50.831
developers are aware of the API
security risks and can create and

00:23:50.831 --> 00:23:53.021
test code to mitigate those risks.

00:23:57.066 --> 00:24:01.806
Where 6.2.1 calls for organizations
to utilize industry best practices

00:24:01.806 --> 00:24:06.116
for secure application development,
6.2.2 goes a step further and

00:24:06.126 --> 00:24:08.026
mandates training for developers.

00:24:08.216 --> 00:24:11.856
It states, " Software development
personnel working on bespoke

00:24:11.856 --> 00:24:15.076
and custom software must be
trained at least every 12 months.

00:24:15.736 --> 00:24:18.123
And that training needs to be: 1.

00:24:18.133 --> 00:24:21.413
Relevant to their job function
and development languages.

00:24:21.963 --> 00:24:22.483
2.

00:24:22.572 --> 00:24:25.222
Include secure software
design and coding techniques.

00:24:25.314 --> 00:24:25.994
And 3.

00:24:26.714 --> 00:24:29.499
Train on the use of security
testing tools for detecting

00:24:29.499 --> 00:24:30.869
vulnerabilities in software."

00:24:31.649 --> 00:24:36.119
As organizations develop applications
with APIs, engineering teams must

00:24:36.119 --> 00:24:40.869
be trained on API related risks as
well as best practices for avoiding

00:24:40.879 --> 00:24:42.759
API exposures and vulnerabilities.

00:24:43.439 --> 00:24:48.419
Organizations should arm development teams
with API-focused security training, such

00:24:48.419 --> 00:24:51.009
as what we provide on APIsec University.

00:24:51.689 --> 00:24:55.219
The standard also points to a
need for vulnerability detection

00:24:55.279 --> 00:24:57.179
and testing custom software.

00:24:57.729 --> 00:25:02.789
For APIs, organizations need to begin
or expand their security testing.

00:25:02.899 --> 00:25:08.634
And as we saw earlier, Only 4% of all
API testing is focused on security.

00:25:09.584 --> 00:25:13.864
New API security testing tools do
exist that will automate API testing

00:25:13.864 --> 00:25:15.954
and give you continuous assessment.

00:25:16.634 --> 00:25:20.784
Organizations can't rely solely
on manual testing or once

00:25:20.784 --> 00:25:22.294
or twice a year pen testing.

00:25:22.944 --> 00:25:23.739
And finally.

00:25:23.919 --> 00:25:26.629
Development teams need to be
trained on these tools and how to

00:25:26.629 --> 00:25:30.239
use them to find vulnerabilities
before they ever get to production.

00:25:30.999 --> 00:25:34.739
I'd like to point out that APIsec
University makes our courses compatible

00:25:34.749 --> 00:25:36.929
with internal Learning Management Systems.

00:25:37.349 --> 00:25:39.889
So if you'd like to provide our
courses to your colleagues and

00:25:39.889 --> 00:25:42.789
employees within your LMS, we can help.

00:25:43.069 --> 00:25:44.239
Feel free to get in touch.

00:25:47.677 --> 00:25:52.087
Section 6.2.3 continues the theme
of looking for and addressing

00:25:52.087 --> 00:25:53.287
vulnerabilities in code.

00:25:54.037 --> 00:25:57.777
" Bespoken custom software must be reviewed
prior to being released into production

00:25:57.847 --> 00:26:01.791
or to customers to identify correct
potential coding vulnerabilities."

00:26:02.621 --> 00:26:06.191
These code reviews need to look for
new and emerging vulnerabilities.

00:26:06.241 --> 00:26:08.721
And traditionally, new and
emerging vulnerabilities refers

00:26:08.721 --> 00:26:12.541
to things like zero-days or other
newly discovered vulnerabilities.

00:26:13.411 --> 00:26:17.631
These vulnerabilities are most likely to
be related to third party libraries and

00:26:17.661 --> 00:26:19.721
APIs that you use in your applications.

00:26:20.431 --> 00:26:23.441
So make sure to keep updated
on any vulnerability reports.

00:26:24.171 --> 00:26:26.911
A critical best practice,
though, when using third party

00:26:26.911 --> 00:26:29.071
APIs is to trust nothing.

00:26:29.691 --> 00:26:32.541
Your application should not assume
that the data coming back from

00:26:32.541 --> 00:26:34.277
external providers is legitimate.

00:26:34.927 --> 00:26:38.667
Check all responses to make sure that
it fits with what you're expecting.

00:26:38.987 --> 00:26:43.941
For example, if your app uses a third
party API to normalize, say, physical

00:26:43.941 --> 00:26:48.656
addresses, make sure you don't accept
responses that could be malicious, like

00:26:48.666 --> 00:26:52.916
URLs, or strings with slashes, or anything
that doesn't look like an address.

00:26:53.896 --> 00:26:57.566
And the final item in the Good Practice
section is especially relevant.

00:26:57.726 --> 00:26:59.616
"Checking for logical vulnerabilities."

00:27:00.146 --> 00:27:03.526
This is perhaps the most dangerous
type of flaw in applications.

00:27:03.676 --> 00:27:08.686
If there is a logic flaw in your app,
that flaw will get exposed via the API,

00:27:08.796 --> 00:27:10.526
allowing an attacker to exploit it.

00:27:11.016 --> 00:27:14.796
Your custom software must be analyzed
and tested to find these logic flaws.

00:27:20.011 --> 00:27:23.251
This section of Requirement 6 is
one of the most substantial in terms

00:27:23.251 --> 00:27:27.561
of testing for vulnerabilities,
and I like to think of 6.2.4 as

00:27:27.581 --> 00:27:30.161
the OWASP section of Requirement 6.

00:27:30.701 --> 00:27:34.581
It mandates the use of "software
engineering techniques to prevent

00:27:34.651 --> 00:27:37.901
or mitigate common software
attacks and vulnerabilities in

00:27:37.911 --> 00:27:39.371
bespoke and custom software."

00:27:40.101 --> 00:27:44.621
So this section details a whole lot of
attack vectors that need to be considered.

00:27:44.661 --> 00:27:49.951
Things like injections, authentication
exploits, bypassing authorization,

00:27:49.991 --> 00:27:53.701
cross site scripting, cross
site request forgery, security

00:27:53.701 --> 00:27:55.361
misconfiguration, and more.

00:27:55.986 --> 00:27:58.866
All of these covered by the OWASP Top 10.

00:27:59.486 --> 00:28:04.036
It also explicitly flags APIs as an
interface that must be considered

00:28:04.036 --> 00:28:05.476
when addressing these risks.

00:28:06.146 --> 00:28:09.356
Your testing programs must
include APIs,  not just the

00:28:09.356 --> 00:28:10.856
web and mobile app interfaces.

00:28:12.036 --> 00:28:16.761
6.2.4 also reinforces the concept of
business logic attacks that we just

00:28:16.761 --> 00:28:21.491
learned about in the prior section and
the need to examine your own application

00:28:21.491 --> 00:28:25.961
functionality and consider not only
how your apps are meant to be used, but

00:28:26.181 --> 00:28:29.301
critically, how your apps might be abused.

00:28:29.851 --> 00:28:33.751
For example, and here's a real
world example, there was an

00:28:33.751 --> 00:28:38.491
attacker who exploited an API on
a crypto trading platform to sell

00:28:38.501 --> 00:28:40.831
his Ethereum stock as Bitcoin.

00:28:42.101 --> 00:28:46.041
The API allowed a user to overwrite
what asset they were trading

00:28:46.051 --> 00:28:47.451
and sell it as something else.

00:28:47.771 --> 00:28:49.491
A great example of a logic flaw.

00:28:50.451 --> 00:28:55.281
Web scanners and pen testers regularly
test UIs for injection vulnerabilities,

00:28:55.671 --> 00:28:59.271
and that must be expanded to
include testing at the API layer.

00:28:59.921 --> 00:29:03.731
In the last callout box here,
you'll see developers should be

00:29:03.731 --> 00:29:06.881
familiar with different types of
vulnerabilities and potential attacks,

00:29:07.241 --> 00:29:11.071
and use measures to avoid potential
attack vectors when developing code.

00:29:11.946 --> 00:29:15.136
The need for security training
for developers repeats

00:29:15.136 --> 00:29:16.846
throughout Requirement 6.

00:29:17.286 --> 00:29:20.456
Again, the API Security Fundamentals
course offers great training

00:29:20.456 --> 00:29:23.816
for developers and others who
need to understand API risks.

00:29:27.504 --> 00:29:31.004
6.3.1 covers Vulnerability
Management and sets the

00:29:31.004 --> 00:29:34.414
requirements for how organizations
need to operate a VM program.

00:29:35.044 --> 00:29:38.984
To start, security teams need to have
access to vulnerability information.

00:29:39.444 --> 00:29:41.324
There are plenty of
vulnerability databases.

00:29:41.664 --> 00:29:44.384
And  the industry standard
source for vulnerabilities is

00:29:44.404 --> 00:29:47.014
the CVE database hosted by MITRE.

00:29:47.414 --> 00:29:50.064
You can find it at cve.mitre.org.

00:29:51.084 --> 00:29:53.954
CVE stands for Common
Vulnerabilities and Exposures.

00:29:53.964 --> 00:29:57.624
And pretty much every reported
vulnerability gets documented here

00:29:57.674 --> 00:30:00.084
and is issued a CVE ID number.

00:30:00.824 --> 00:30:04.634
But what you'll find here, as the name
states, are common vulnerabilities.

00:30:05.434 --> 00:30:05.654
I.

00:30:05.654 --> 00:30:10.094
e., vulnerabilities in third party
code, apps, and libraries that you use.

00:30:10.814 --> 00:30:14.414
But notice the statement here
at the bottom to specifically

00:30:14.414 --> 00:30:17.954
look for vulnerabilities in
custom code and bespoke software.

00:30:18.794 --> 00:30:23.264
And here, the DSS adds more detail
about where to find vulnerabilities

00:30:23.284 --> 00:30:25.084
in your third party software.

00:30:26.144 --> 00:30:29.344
And all of this so far really
focuses on the third party.

00:30:30.004 --> 00:30:34.294
But here in the last callout box,
you'll see DSS showing concern for

00:30:34.294 --> 00:30:36.344
your proprietary application logic.

00:30:37.329 --> 00:30:39.079
And this is a really critical element.

00:30:39.379 --> 00:30:43.619
DSS states, "for control over
in house developed software, the

00:30:43.619 --> 00:30:47.429
organization may receive such
information from external sources."

00:30:48.159 --> 00:30:51.019
And then they highlight bug
bounty programs, independent

00:30:51.029 --> 00:30:52.439
investigators, and more.

00:30:53.429 --> 00:30:56.889
Now, this requirement has big
implications for your APIs.

00:30:57.039 --> 00:31:01.639
First, you need to secure the third party
APIs that your applications consume.

00:31:02.349 --> 00:31:05.869
That means keeping up to date on
versions, patching that's required,

00:31:06.279 --> 00:31:08.009
monitoring for new vulnerabilities.

00:31:08.599 --> 00:31:12.159
And second, you need to look at
your internally generated code,

00:31:12.249 --> 00:31:16.849
including anything that's exposed
by APIs to find vulnerabilities or

00:31:16.849 --> 00:31:21.064
logic flaws, authorization gaps or
anything else that can be exploited.

00:31:21.684 --> 00:31:25.524
And you're not going to find
this in any CVE database or Patch

00:31:25.524 --> 00:31:27.214
Tuesday alert or anything else.

00:31:27.684 --> 00:31:31.154
Flaws in your code are your
own personal zero-days.

00:31:31.774 --> 00:31:34.584
And these vulnerabilities are
the types of vulnerabilities

00:31:34.604 --> 00:31:36.314
attackers most want to find.

00:31:37.134 --> 00:31:39.489
The traditional and common
vulnerabilities, things like injections

00:31:39.489 --> 00:31:45.104
and cross site scripting, are rarely
the cause of major API breaches.

00:31:46.004 --> 00:31:50.994
Your APIs expose your application
logic, the good and the bad, and

00:31:50.994 --> 00:31:54.134
any flaws in your application
logic will also get exposed.

00:31:54.544 --> 00:31:56.814
So it's critical to, 1.

00:31:57.224 --> 00:32:00.744
keep up to date on the latest
API attack techniques, and 2.

00:32:01.254 --> 00:32:04.134
perform security testing on
your applications regularly.

00:32:04.704 --> 00:32:08.414
That means not once or twice a
year, but with every release.

00:32:08.484 --> 00:32:12.046
And make sure that testing covers your
APIs and is  providing adequate coverage.

00:32:12.136 --> 00:32:15.402
That means testing every endpoint
across dozens of attack vectors,

00:32:15.432 --> 00:32:17.612
including logic and authorization tests.

00:32:18.452 --> 00:32:22.512
Now, this probably sounds a bit daunting,
and the only way to achieve this level of

00:32:22.512 --> 00:32:24.642
continuous assessment is with automation.

00:32:24.722 --> 00:32:27.172
There are API optimized testing
tools out there to help.

00:32:31.253 --> 00:32:37.063
6.3.2 focuses on having an up to date,
complete, and accurate software inventory,

00:32:37.563 --> 00:32:42.093
specifically your bespoke and custom
software, as well as any third party code

00:32:42.103 --> 00:32:44.113
you're using in your custom applications.

00:32:44.673 --> 00:32:49.023
The stated goal of this inventory
is to "facilitate vulnerability

00:32:49.023 --> 00:32:49.923
and patch management."

00:32:50.923 --> 00:32:55.533
And here in the Purpose section,
the DSS explicitly calls out

00:32:55.563 --> 00:32:57.423
third party libraries and APIs.

00:32:57.593 --> 00:33:01.073
These need to be known, tracked,
and managed to address any

00:33:01.073 --> 00:33:02.463
vulnerabilities that they might have.

00:33:03.473 --> 00:33:08.063
Actually, having an accurate inventory
of your software and APIs isn't just

00:33:08.073 --> 00:33:11.803
good for security, it's critical to
your overall application governance.

00:33:12.333 --> 00:33:16.063
I've met with many security teams who
claim they have little awareness of all

00:33:16.063 --> 00:33:19.653
the APIs running in their organization,
and this is undoubtedly true.

00:33:20.363 --> 00:33:23.013
But the fact that security teams
don't have this information

00:33:23.053 --> 00:33:24.613
doesn't mean it doesn't exist.

00:33:25.193 --> 00:33:29.473
So security teams have to partner
with Engineering to deal with

00:33:29.473 --> 00:33:31.233
these API security concerns.

00:33:31.693 --> 00:33:35.533
The reason why is APIs are
fundamentally gateways to your

00:33:35.533 --> 00:33:36.903
application functionality.

00:33:37.423 --> 00:33:41.873
And most vulnerabilities will need to
get addressed inside the app code in

00:33:41.873 --> 00:33:43.363
collaboration with the engineering team.

00:33:44.328 --> 00:33:47.968
We've got whole courses dedicated
to API governance, but that's really

00:33:47.968 --> 00:33:49.648
where security needs to start.

00:33:50.288 --> 00:33:55.148
Governance means knowing what APIs you
have, but also what function they provide,

00:33:55.158 --> 00:33:59.528
who has access to them, how they need to
be secured, things like authentication,

00:33:59.558 --> 00:34:02.238
access controls, input validation, etc.

00:34:03.788 --> 00:34:07.498
And governance also means setting
standards for how your APIs are

00:34:07.498 --> 00:34:10.728
documented, what policies you
have for handling sensitive data,

00:34:11.113 --> 00:34:14.033
when you need new versions, what
happens to the old versions.

00:34:15.253 --> 00:34:18.993
And governance also means APIs
get deployed into production in a

00:34:18.993 --> 00:34:22.373
standard way where every release
goes through, not just functional

00:34:22.373 --> 00:34:24.743
testing, but security testing too.

00:34:25.013 --> 00:34:28.763
And all APIs get managed in a common
platform, like an API gateway.

00:34:29.403 --> 00:34:33.013
This gives your organization, not
just great consistency and operational

00:34:33.013 --> 00:34:36.173
efficiency, but also enables security.

00:34:36.413 --> 00:34:40.053
What APIs you have, how they're being
used, and they have to clear the

00:34:40.053 --> 00:34:42.013
necessary gates to get into production.

00:34:43.223 --> 00:34:48.593
For this requirement, organizations
have until March 31, 2025, one year

00:34:48.593 --> 00:34:51.353
later than most of the DSS 4.0.

00:34:52.173 --> 00:34:55.413
And that's likely a reflection of
the challenges organizations have in

00:34:55.413 --> 00:34:57.463
even producing an accurate inventory.

00:34:57.983 --> 00:35:01.183
Until then, DSS calls this
a best practice, which I

00:35:01.183 --> 00:35:02.083
couldn't agree more with.

00:35:02.573 --> 00:35:06.318
Definitely invest the effort
to produce an inventory.

00:35:06.868 --> 00:35:09.828
But if you're going to do this,
then make the most out of it.

00:35:10.308 --> 00:35:13.108
Use this as a driver for
better overall security in your

00:35:13.108 --> 00:35:17.018
organization and better governance
across the entire API lifecycle.

00:35:17.018 --> 00:35:22.818
Look at your API program from design to
coding to testing to deployment to really

00:35:22.828 --> 00:35:25.256
reap the benefits of better governance.

00:35:29.419 --> 00:35:34.129
We're next going to cover 6.4.1 and 6.4.2
together, and you'll see why in a second.

00:35:34.909 --> 00:35:38.519
This requirement addresses the need
to actively protect public facing

00:35:38.519 --> 00:35:40.299
web applications against attacks.

00:35:40.919 --> 00:35:45.159
The purpose, of course, is to make
sure applications are resilient against

00:35:45.159 --> 00:35:50.349
attacks, as web applications are going to
be the primary target for those attacks.

00:35:50.949 --> 00:35:55.212
As DSS states, "poorly coded
web applications provide an easy

00:35:55.212 --> 00:35:58.182
path for attackers to gain access
to sensitive data and systems."

00:35:59.212 --> 00:36:01.892
But pay attention to that
term web application.

00:36:01.982 --> 00:36:06.222
Many will interpret that as
referring to their web, and

00:36:06.222 --> 00:36:07.962
maybe mobile, user interfaces.

00:36:08.782 --> 00:36:11.622
When we think of an application,
we tend to picture something you

00:36:11.622 --> 00:36:13.452
can look at and interact with.

00:36:14.022 --> 00:36:16.482
But this is not the full
scope of your application.

00:36:16.522 --> 00:36:20.722
You have to look at any interface,
human or machine, that can

00:36:20.722 --> 00:36:21.834
exercise your application.

00:36:22.144 --> 00:36:23.534
And that includes APIs.

00:36:24.864 --> 00:36:28.784
So the choices DSS provides here are
to either perform a vulnerability

00:36:28.784 --> 00:36:34.394
assessment or implement some kind of
inline automated protection solution.

00:36:35.144 --> 00:36:38.824
If you go with the vulnerability
assessment route, the requirement is to

00:36:38.824 --> 00:36:43.164
perform the scan every 12 months or after
any major change to your application.

00:36:43.864 --> 00:36:47.264
The assessment must be performed
by an organization that specializes

00:36:47.284 --> 00:36:48.834
in application security.

00:36:49.204 --> 00:36:53.044
It has to cover all the threats
that we talked about in 6.2.4.

00:36:53.814 --> 00:36:58.189
You have to rank all the vulnerabilities
you find and correct them, as specified in

00:36:58.189 --> 00:37:02.789
6.3.1, and then retest your apps to make
sure the vulnerabilities are remedied.

00:37:03.579 --> 00:37:07.849
Now for the automated protection approach,
this has to be installed in front of

00:37:07.849 --> 00:37:11.689
your web app, running continuously
to find and block any attacks.

00:37:12.169 --> 00:37:15.849
And it must produce audit logs that
can be analyzed later as needed.

00:37:16.504 --> 00:37:19.744
The most common example of an
automated protection solution is

00:37:19.784 --> 00:37:22.054
a WAF, a Web Application Firewall.

00:37:22.684 --> 00:37:25.664
These are widely used to analyze
application layer traffic and

00:37:25.664 --> 00:37:29.824
block against known threats like
injections, high volume attacks,

00:37:29.824 --> 00:37:31.644
cross site scripting, etc.

00:37:32.663 --> 00:37:37.211
An API gateway will also provide
protection and can perform things like

00:37:37.211 --> 00:37:43.351
API input validation, user authentication,
access controls, rate limiting, IP

00:37:43.351 --> 00:37:45.281
and geographic whitelisting and more.

00:37:46.451 --> 00:37:49.561
There are API specific runtime
protection solutions as well

00:37:49.571 --> 00:37:51.981
that act like WAFs for APIs.

00:37:52.979 --> 00:37:56.829
In terms of timing, 6.4.1 gives
organizations a choice of approaches,

00:37:57.099 --> 00:38:01.109
but you have to comply with one
or the other by March 31, 2024.

00:38:01.939 --> 00:38:08.089
Now, under 6.4.2, that option goes away,
and you must deploy an automated approach,

00:38:08.139 --> 00:38:13.799
or an automated technical solution,
and that is due by March 31, 2025.

00:38:14.679 --> 00:38:18.149
Now, although runtime detection and
blocking of active threats is very

00:38:18.149 --> 00:38:22.309
appealing, the reality is certain
attacks are nearly impossible to

00:38:22.309 --> 00:38:25.870
detect in real time, as signature
based blocking and anomaly detection

00:38:25.930 --> 00:38:28.140
aren't able to identify every attack.

00:38:28.840 --> 00:38:32.860
Some of the most damaging API attacks
we've seen exploit unique application

00:38:32.860 --> 00:38:35.360
logic where unintended flaws exist.

00:38:36.040 --> 00:38:39.130
In the example I gave earlier
with the crypto trading platform,

00:38:39.200 --> 00:38:42.380
the user exploited a flaw in the
API where they could change their

00:38:42.390 --> 00:38:44.270
asset from one type to another.

00:38:44.890 --> 00:38:48.240
This would be virtually impossible
to detect in any automated way.

00:38:48.790 --> 00:38:51.130
So really the best practice here is both.

00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:55.670
To do both automated blocking where
possible, and to complement that

00:38:55.670 --> 00:38:59.320
with comprehensive testing to uncover
flaws before attackers find them.

00:39:04.044 --> 00:39:09.086
Section 7 of the DSS focuses on access
controls to critical data and systems.

00:39:09.686 --> 00:39:12.386
I just want to touch on this section
in general and highlight a few

00:39:12.386 --> 00:39:13.926
requirements and best practices.

00:39:14.786 --> 00:39:18.506
The purpose of this whole
Requirement 7 boils down to who

00:39:18.516 --> 00:39:21.606
has access to applications and
systems and what can they do.

00:39:22.536 --> 00:39:25.046
Of course, this applies to the
underlying infrastructure that

00:39:25.046 --> 00:39:26.456
supports your applications.

00:39:26.496 --> 00:39:29.596
The servers, containers,
databases, network devices.

00:39:30.516 --> 00:39:33.496
You have to make sure access is
secure and controlled internally.

00:39:34.476 --> 00:39:37.266
At the application layer, though,
you have to think about all the

00:39:37.266 --> 00:39:41.386
functionality, the data accessibility,
the business flows, and your user types.

00:39:41.856 --> 00:39:45.756
For example, a regular user should be
able to access their account balance.

00:39:46.086 --> 00:39:48.716
They should not be able to
see any other user's data.

00:39:49.326 --> 00:39:52.446
But maybe you have support
personnel who need to be able to

00:39:52.456 --> 00:39:54.256
access any user's information.

00:39:54.991 --> 00:39:57.261
Or take that end user
accessing their balance.

00:39:57.301 --> 00:39:59.871
They should be able to see their
balance, but not modify it.

00:40:00.701 --> 00:40:04.311
So what this means is, you need to
build a matrix of all functionality

00:40:04.311 --> 00:40:08.701
available through your APIs, and map that
across all of your user types, whether

00:40:08.701 --> 00:40:14.051
that's an end user, support person,
QA engineer, developer, admin, etc.

00:40:14.771 --> 00:40:18.011
And even map that across your
different environments, development,

00:40:18.021 --> 00:40:19.641
testing, staging, production.

00:40:20.461 --> 00:40:23.301
It's not enough to do this
analysis at the UI level.

00:40:23.771 --> 00:40:28.271
As we've seen before, while UIs provide
the ability to control user behavior,

00:40:28.621 --> 00:40:33.591
filter data, and manage what they can do,
when you take the UI out of the equation,

00:40:33.611 --> 00:40:36.221
you're left with individual API endpoints.

00:40:36.221 --> 00:40:39.691
So make sure you're analyzing
access controls at the API level.

00:40:40.761 --> 00:40:44.521
Now let's talk for a second about
authentication versus authorization.

00:40:45.306 --> 00:40:49.556
You definitely want to set up policies
around API endpoint authentication and

00:40:49.556 --> 00:40:54.088
define those policies in an API Style
Guide that all your developers can use.

00:40:54.518 --> 00:40:58.758
For example, if an endpoint is
purely informational, has no user

00:40:58.758 --> 00:41:02.228
data, for example, an endpoint that
checks where the nearest bank branch

00:41:02.228 --> 00:41:05.128
is, then maybe you don't need any
authentication on that endpoint.

00:41:05.788 --> 00:41:08.568
But another endpoint could let a
user check their account balance.

00:41:08.638 --> 00:41:10.188
So you'll want to authenticate them.

00:41:10.608 --> 00:41:13.908
And maybe even higher security
for performing a transfer, like

00:41:13.918 --> 00:41:15.488
putting in a two factor challenge.

00:41:16.228 --> 00:41:19.418
That's all about confirming that
the user is who they say they are.

00:41:19.758 --> 00:41:21.918
And this can be managed at the API layer.

00:41:22.378 --> 00:41:26.348
And the best practice is to centralize
all that access in an API gateway.

00:41:27.298 --> 00:41:30.838
But authentication is not the same
as authorization, and we've seen

00:41:30.838 --> 00:41:34.968
many breaches where an organization
implemented user authentication but

00:41:34.968 --> 00:41:36.788
failed to address authorization.

00:41:37.638 --> 00:41:38.907
Let's use another example.

00:41:38.987 --> 00:41:42.497
There's a popular exercise equipment
vendor that provides an API that has

00:41:42.497 --> 00:41:44.564
access to all users in the platform.

00:41:45.204 --> 00:41:48.664
Although the API required users
to authenticate properly to access

00:41:48.694 --> 00:41:51.844
the endpoints, it did not control
which records they could see.

00:41:52.424 --> 00:41:56.574
So once authenticated, the user could
see not only their own records, they

00:41:56.574 --> 00:41:58.259
could see anyone else's as well.

00:41:58.989 --> 00:42:00.569
This is more common
than you might imagine.

00:42:01.449 --> 00:42:05.339
The point is authorization needs
to be considered and defined

00:42:05.669 --> 00:42:09.749
and then implemented within the
application logic itself, not at

00:42:09.749 --> 00:42:11.489
a gateway or other access point.

00:42:12.619 --> 00:42:17.439
Lastly, once you've got your access
controls defined and implemented properly.

00:42:17.849 --> 00:42:19.579
You want to make sure nothing changes.

00:42:19.799 --> 00:42:21.649
And if something does, you know about it.

00:42:22.439 --> 00:42:25.109
Here's where we recommend
RBAC monitoring, i.

00:42:25.159 --> 00:42:25.329
e.

00:42:25.329 --> 00:42:29.299
continuous testing of Role Based
Access Controls, to see if any settings

00:42:29.299 --> 00:42:33.139
get adjusted so you can be aware
and ensure no control gaps emerge.

00:42:39.551 --> 00:42:41.521
Here's another section
I'll cover at a high level.

00:42:41.711 --> 00:42:46.481
10.0, "Log and Monitor All Access to
System Components and Cardholder Data."

00:42:47.401 --> 00:42:50.801
This section spells out in detail all
the requirements for collecting logs,

00:42:50.861 --> 00:42:54.941
storing the data in a central repository,
and analyzing the activity records for

00:42:54.951 --> 00:42:57.421
security and incident response purposes.

00:42:58.071 --> 00:43:02.321
As the DSS states, "logs allow
thorough tracking, alerting, and

00:43:02.321 --> 00:43:04.181
analysis when something does go wrong.

00:43:04.521 --> 00:43:08.101
Determining the cause of a compromise
is difficult, if not impossible,

00:43:08.111 --> 00:43:09.881
without system activity logs."

00:43:10.811 --> 00:43:15.381
Now typically logging is enabled
and captured at the system level, i.

00:43:15.441 --> 00:43:15.591
e.

00:43:15.591 --> 00:43:19.461
all the servers, databases, network
devices, firewalls, and more.

00:43:20.341 --> 00:43:23.806
But logging must also be captured
at the application level.

00:43:24.006 --> 00:43:27.616
Here's where you'll see the
details of user behavior and be

00:43:27.616 --> 00:43:29.326
able to find anomalous activity.

00:43:30.191 --> 00:43:34.491
To get this level of granularity, your
application must first be instrumented

00:43:34.531 --> 00:43:36.461
to produce the logs in the first place.

00:43:37.301 --> 00:43:40.691
Make sure your developers add
logging events into their code that

00:43:40.721 --> 00:43:42.491
captures all the relevant details.

00:43:42.741 --> 00:43:44.261
Who's performing the action?

00:43:44.271 --> 00:43:45.241
What are they doing?

00:43:45.251 --> 00:43:46.321
When did it occur?

00:43:46.821 --> 00:43:48.991
From what source or IP address?

00:43:49.001 --> 00:43:50.541
Was the action successful?

00:43:51.471 --> 00:43:55.871
Another great source of application
level logging is at the API Gateway.

00:43:56.231 --> 00:44:01.841
These platforms will natively record all
accesses to APIs and individual endpoints.

00:44:02.701 --> 00:44:06.171
Finally, be sure to centralize all
this data in a common repository

00:44:06.531 --> 00:44:09.731
where your security team can analyze
and detect threats as they happen.

00:44:15.396 --> 00:44:20.016
And the last section I want to cover
in PCI DSS 4.0 is Requirement 11.

00:44:20.566 --> 00:44:23.076
"Test security of systems
and networks regularly."

00:44:23.776 --> 00:44:27.596
Throughout this course, and across
many of the DSS 4.0 sections, we've

00:44:27.606 --> 00:44:29.456
talked about how important testing is.

00:44:30.236 --> 00:44:34.846
Many of the DSS requirements specify
security objectives, with the need

00:44:34.846 --> 00:44:38.376
to test to confirm programs are
running as designed, to ensure

00:44:38.386 --> 00:44:42.406
vulnerabilities aren't appearing,
and access controls are set properly.

00:44:43.316 --> 00:44:48.436
Section 11.0 formalizes this need
to test for security regularly, and

00:44:48.446 --> 00:44:53.406
DSS recognizes that "vulnerabilities
are being discovered continually by

00:44:53.406 --> 00:44:56.747
malicious individuals and researchers,
and being introduced by new software.

00:44:57.397 --> 00:45:01.137
System components, processes, and
bespoke and custom software should

00:45:01.147 --> 00:45:04.340
be tested frequently to ensure
security controls continue to

00:45:04.340 --> 00:45:05.804
reflect a changing environment."

00:45:06.694 --> 00:45:10.834
As you can see, this requirement
specifically refers to bespoke and

00:45:10.834 --> 00:45:14.544
custom software and the vulnerabilities
that your code can create.

00:45:15.344 --> 00:45:19.554
It also mandates organizations test
applications frequently to ensure

00:45:19.554 --> 00:45:21.384
no vulnerabilities go undiscovered.

00:45:22.124 --> 00:45:24.724
As we've discussed throughout
this course, application testing

00:45:24.724 --> 00:45:26.624
cannot be limited to UI alone.

00:45:27.154 --> 00:45:31.404
Public facing applications rely
on public facing APIs, and these

00:45:31.404 --> 00:45:33.144
are ripe targets for attackers.

00:45:33.644 --> 00:45:37.874
So your testing programs must include
APIs and should be automated to ensure

00:45:37.874 --> 00:45:42.304
complete coverage of every API endpoint,
simulate attacks across all of the

00:45:42.314 --> 00:45:44.784
OWASP Top 10 categories and more.

00:45:45.384 --> 00:45:48.744
And run in a continuous
manner, recognizing that modern

00:45:48.744 --> 00:45:52.324
organizations update code
monthly, weekly, and even daily.

00:45:52.694 --> 00:45:54.394
So make sure your testing keeps up.

00:45:59.194 --> 00:46:02.854
Okay, we've covered a lot of ground
here and have examined how organizations

00:46:02.854 --> 00:46:07.874
need to address API security concerns
to stay compliant with PCI DSS 4.0.

00:46:08.454 --> 00:46:11.364
So let's bring it all together and
summarize what the requirements

00:46:11.364 --> 00:46:14.414
are, some of the best practices
for not only complying with the

00:46:14.414 --> 00:46:17.844
standard, but in elevating your
organization's security posture.

00:46:18.334 --> 00:46:20.824
And we'll wrap it up with
a list of do's and don'ts.

00:46:22.034 --> 00:46:25.574
First, let's summarize the
various sections of DSS 4.0

00:46:25.574 --> 00:46:27.034
and what they mean for APIs.

00:46:28.059 --> 00:46:33.989
Starting with 2.2.7, this requires secure
access to admin consoles, including

00:46:33.989 --> 00:46:35.459
encryption of all communications.

00:46:35.939 --> 00:46:39.789
Make sure you're looking at API access
to consoles as well, and not only

00:46:39.789 --> 00:46:43.549
encrypting the communication, but
controlling access to those endpoints.

00:46:44.559 --> 00:46:50.564
6.0 is the core of DSS 4.0 with respect
to securing payment related applications.

00:46:51.094 --> 00:46:54.824
We spent a lot of time looking at many
different subsections of Requirement 6.

00:46:55.324 --> 00:47:01.364
The key to remember here is that with
DSS 4.0, APIs are 100% in scope now.

00:47:01.574 --> 00:47:04.984
All your bespoke and custom
software, the code you write to

00:47:04.984 --> 00:47:08.564
power your organization's unique
offerings, is powered by APIs.

00:47:08.814 --> 00:47:12.924
And those APIs need to be secured
as much, or even more than your

00:47:12.924 --> 00:47:15.274
classic web and mobile applications.

00:47:15.944 --> 00:47:19.394
The subsections in requirement 6
get more specific about securing

00:47:19.394 --> 00:47:20.764
your custom applications.

00:47:21.604 --> 00:47:26.074
6.2.1 focuses on secure software
development, and requires

00:47:26.084 --> 00:47:29.314
engineers to be aware of API
security and vulnerability risks.

00:47:29.494 --> 00:47:32.104
to ensure code is free of
flaws that can be exploited.

00:47:32.704 --> 00:47:36.914
DSS recommends integrating as far left
as possible to find vulnerabilities

00:47:36.914 --> 00:47:38.294
before they reach production.

00:47:39.234 --> 00:47:42.684
6.2.2 sets requirements for
annual training of developers.

00:47:43.604 --> 00:47:46.534
APIs have unique attack patterns, so
it's critical that your developers

00:47:46.554 --> 00:47:50.414
understand what these look like and
how to create more secure applications.

00:47:51.264 --> 00:47:55.684
6.2.3 requires organizations to review
and test code for security flaws.

00:47:56.174 --> 00:47:59.834
Here, DSS highlights how attackers
are targeting your custom code

00:48:00.209 --> 00:48:04.129
via the APIs, and looking to find
and exploit logic vulnerabilities.

00:48:04.879 --> 00:48:09.509
6.2.4 covers a wide range of well known
application attack types and mirrors

00:48:09.509 --> 00:48:14.499
closely the threats listed in the OWASP
application and API security top 10 lists.

00:48:15.099 --> 00:48:19.339
This section rightly calls out API
specifically and highlights attacks on

00:48:19.339 --> 00:48:22.144
business logic and manipulation of APIs.

00:48:22.634 --> 00:48:25.454
The point being, your vulnerability
discovery efforts need to

00:48:25.454 --> 00:48:27.844
cover APIs, not just UIs.

00:48:28.844 --> 00:48:33.334
6.3.1 calls on organizations to implement
vulnerability management programs.

00:48:33.794 --> 00:48:35.844
It's important to focus on
the vulnerabilities that are

00:48:35.844 --> 00:48:37.524
likely to cause the most damage.

00:48:37.959 --> 00:48:41.509
And while injections and cross site
scripting are legitimate vulnerabilities,

00:48:41.509 --> 00:48:45.209
and you absolutely need to test
and fix these flaws, these rarely

00:48:45.229 --> 00:48:46.819
are the cause of major breaches.

00:48:47.349 --> 00:48:50.859
What we see regularly are logic
flaws, weak authentication,

00:48:51.099 --> 00:48:52.499
and authorization gaps.

00:48:52.859 --> 00:48:56.759
These are the vulnerabilities you need
to test for, especially at the API level.

00:48:57.909 --> 00:49:02.569
6.3.2 requires organizations to develop
and maintain an accurate and up to

00:49:02.569 --> 00:49:04.859
date inventory of all custom software.

00:49:05.449 --> 00:49:07.339
This must include your APIs.

00:49:07.889 --> 00:49:11.869
Having that accurate inventory is really
part of a solid application governance

00:49:11.879 --> 00:49:16.949
program where you standardize how APIs
get created, set security policies that

00:49:16.949 --> 00:49:21.839
are implemented consistently and all APIs
get tested and deployed in a common way.

00:49:22.989 --> 00:49:27.899
6.4.1 gives organizations an option to
perform either periodic vulnerability

00:49:27.899 --> 00:49:32.029
assessments or implement runtime
attack detection and blocking.

00:49:32.769 --> 00:49:37.809
The option goes away in 2025 when
all PCI covered entities must enforce

00:49:37.849 --> 00:49:39.379
active detection and blocking.

00:49:39.969 --> 00:49:43.159
Just be aware that not all threats
can be detected in real time.

00:49:43.589 --> 00:49:46.609
There's no magic firewall that
will protect against all threats,

00:49:46.789 --> 00:49:50.949
and in fact, frequently the most
damaging exploits aren't detectable.

00:49:51.429 --> 00:49:54.349
So you need to keep up testing
and detection of vulnerabilities,

00:49:54.389 --> 00:49:55.869
and then fix this in the code.

00:49:57.289 --> 00:49:59.789
7.0 requires strong access controls.

00:50:00.284 --> 00:50:03.384
Here you need to examine the entire
infrastructure and application

00:50:03.384 --> 00:50:06.874
stack and look at what users can
access and what they can see.

00:50:07.665 --> 00:50:11.605
It's important to review these controls
down to the individual API endpoint level

00:50:11.825 --> 00:50:13.915
to make sure there aren't any access gaps.

00:50:14.385 --> 00:50:17.915
And definitely implement continuous
testing to uncover and alert on

00:50:17.935 --> 00:50:19.995
any changes in access permissions.

00:50:21.195 --> 00:50:24.685
10.0 requires organizations to
capture logs from all systems

00:50:24.685 --> 00:50:26.235
involved in payment processing.

00:50:26.695 --> 00:50:30.055
This is critical to detect threats
and perform root cause analysis.

00:50:30.535 --> 00:50:34.685
Make sure you're capturing user activity
logs on APIs as well, and this is

00:50:34.685 --> 00:50:36.675
best accomplished with an API gateway.

00:50:37.675 --> 00:50:42.358
And 11.0 calls on organizations to perform
regular testing of their custom software.

00:50:42.668 --> 00:50:45.118
So make sure that includes
all of your APIs as well.

00:50:49.175 --> 00:50:52.456
All right, as we wrap up this course,
let's touch on some of the core best

00:50:52.466 --> 00:50:56.838
practices, not just to comply with
PCI, but to run a more efficient

00:50:56.838 --> 00:50:59.008
and effective API security program.

00:50:59.788 --> 00:51:00.948
We'll start with governance.

00:51:01.508 --> 00:51:04.638
API security is not really
something you can bolt on.

00:51:04.708 --> 00:51:08.598
You want to integrate security into
the entire application lifecycle, from

00:51:08.598 --> 00:51:12.708
design, to product, to retirement,
creating a culture of security

00:51:12.708 --> 00:51:13.978
throughout your organization.

00:51:14.583 --> 00:51:18.503
This starts with putting time and
effort into defining your API policies,

00:51:18.543 --> 00:51:23.903
how your APIs get used, who can use
them, what they can do, then defining

00:51:23.903 --> 00:51:27.893
your standards, what APIs should
look like, how do they get created.

00:51:28.463 --> 00:51:30.333
And then standardizing your processes.

00:51:31.098 --> 00:51:34.688
How do APIs move from development
through QA and into production

00:51:34.698 --> 00:51:35.808
in a consistent manner.

00:51:36.378 --> 00:51:39.488
It's highly recommended that you
centralize all your APIs in a

00:51:39.488 --> 00:51:43.098
common platform, something like
an API gateway or a marketplace.

00:51:43.648 --> 00:51:47.378
This will let you manage APIs
consistently, define common policies,

00:51:47.768 --> 00:51:51.398
and give you a central source for
auditing and logging user behavior.

00:51:52.103 --> 00:51:55.393
And make sure all your APIs only go
into production having passed the

00:51:55.393 --> 00:52:00.683
required gates, including documentation,
testing, security audit, and so forth.

00:52:01.753 --> 00:52:05.753
Our next best practice focuses on how to
create a culture of security within the

00:52:05.753 --> 00:52:07.913
engineering and development organizations.

00:52:08.713 --> 00:52:11.383
We've hosted many roundtables
with cybersecurity leaders

00:52:11.383 --> 00:52:12.583
and noticed a common theme.

00:52:12.908 --> 00:52:16.718
The CISOs uniformly agreed on
the need to integrate security

00:52:16.738 --> 00:52:18.228
into the development team.

00:52:18.708 --> 00:52:22.928
And one of the best ways to create that
security awareness was to embed security

00:52:22.928 --> 00:52:24.748
champions right into those teams.

00:52:25.478 --> 00:52:29.888
As we've discussed, especially with
applications and APIs, development

00:52:29.888 --> 00:52:33.548
shares the responsibility for
security, as most vulnerabilities will

00:52:33.548 --> 00:52:35.648
require dev involvement to remediate.

00:52:36.288 --> 00:52:40.178
Security champions are security
savvy personnel who can work

00:52:40.178 --> 00:52:42.898
and operate directly within
the development organization,

00:52:43.268 --> 00:52:46.868
providing insights, best practices,
answering questions, and so forth.

00:52:48.188 --> 00:52:51.328
Number three on our best practices
list is actually straight out of the

00:52:51.618 --> 00:52:55.898
DSS document: providing API security
training to all relevant employees.

00:52:56.228 --> 00:52:59.868
And this should include any
developers creating APIs, any API

00:52:59.928 --> 00:53:01.818
product owners, and ops teams.

00:53:02.378 --> 00:53:06.258
As we've seen, APIs face new and different
types of attacks, so make sure the

00:53:06.258 --> 00:53:11.148
training covers API specific threats,
the OWASP API Security Top 10, and

00:53:11.158 --> 00:53:13.478
best practices for keeping APIs secure.

00:53:14.158 --> 00:53:16.768
We've got a great course
at APIsec University called

00:53:17.028 --> 00:53:18.538
API Security Fundamentals.

00:53:18.938 --> 00:53:21.948
It covers everything I just mentioned
and can even be deployed in an

00:53:21.948 --> 00:53:23.768
internal Learning Management System.

00:53:24.653 --> 00:53:27.333
And our last best practice
here focuses on testing.

00:53:27.893 --> 00:53:31.273
APIs expose so much application
functionality, and it's more

00:53:31.273 --> 00:53:35.153
critical than ever to really pressure
test that functionality to make

00:53:35.153 --> 00:53:37.173
sure your apps can't be abused.

00:53:37.803 --> 00:53:42.053
This means testing every single API
endpoint across all kinds of attack types,

00:53:42.373 --> 00:53:47.313
everything you see in the OWASP Top 10,
and examine your app logic to look for

00:53:47.313 --> 00:53:49.473
any flaws or gaps that can be exploited.

00:53:50.013 --> 00:53:51.643
And you need to do this with automation.

00:53:51.903 --> 00:53:55.463
Implement a testing program that will
run automatically, create the test

00:53:55.473 --> 00:53:59.153
coverage you need, and run against
every release to find vulnerabilities

00:53:59.153 --> 00:54:00.513
before they get to production.

00:54:04.771 --> 00:54:08.291
I'd like to wrap up my courses with some
really tactical do's and don'ts that you

00:54:08.291 --> 00:54:10.091
can take back and implement right away.

00:54:10.751 --> 00:54:14.471
As we've mentioned a number of
times, DO educate your development

00:54:14.471 --> 00:54:16.751
team and API owners on API risks.

00:54:16.901 --> 00:54:19.161
They're under attack, and the
threats are different than

00:54:19.161 --> 00:54:20.411
what our web apps have seen.

00:54:21.176 --> 00:54:24.446
DO integrate security
into the dev process.

00:54:24.696 --> 00:54:28.246
Make sure engineers understand how to
code more resilient APIs and test for

00:54:28.256 --> 00:54:29.936
vulnerabilities ahead of production.

00:54:30.766 --> 00:54:33.536
DO build communication between
security and development.

00:54:33.726 --> 00:54:36.486
The gap here is way
too big, way too often.

00:54:37.006 --> 00:54:39.686
Both teams need to collaborate
to keep APIs secure.

00:54:40.476 --> 00:54:43.286
DO mandate documentation on all APIs.

00:54:43.906 --> 00:54:46.376
This feels like one that
shouldn't be needed, but far too

00:54:46.376 --> 00:54:48.146
many APIs are not documented.

00:54:48.531 --> 00:54:50.791
Under documented or not updated.

00:54:51.411 --> 00:54:54.541
Documentation helps drive
consistency, reusability,

00:54:54.551 --> 00:54:56.631
integrations, and also security.

00:54:57.756 --> 00:55:00.646
DON'T treat internal APIs
as inherently secure.

00:55:01.306 --> 00:55:04.316
They may be harder to find, but
attackers are pretty clever, and

00:55:04.316 --> 00:55:07.916
we've seen many internal APIs
get accessed in unexpected ways.

00:55:08.836 --> 00:55:12.766
DO centralize API management and
control in something like a gateway.

00:55:13.386 --> 00:55:16.516
Many organizations end up using
multiple gateways, which is fine.

00:55:16.906 --> 00:55:20.456
Just use some platform to manage
all your APIs in a consistent way.

00:55:21.776 --> 00:55:24.766
DON'T rely exclusively on
manual security testing.

00:55:25.146 --> 00:55:28.416
A once or twice a year penetration
test will not keep up with

00:55:28.416 --> 00:55:29.966
the pace of your code changes.

00:55:30.286 --> 00:55:33.006
And it won't give you the breadth
of coverage considering how many

00:55:33.006 --> 00:55:35.986
endpoints you have and the attack
types you need to simulate.

00:55:36.896 --> 00:55:40.109
Which leads to our next
item, DO automate testing.

00:55:40.356 --> 00:55:43.036
Integrate your testing
with your CI/CD pipeline.

00:55:43.046 --> 00:55:45.926
Make sure every release gets
tested before they go live.

00:55:46.601 --> 00:55:48.501
DON'T filter data in the UI.

00:55:48.701 --> 00:55:50.341
This is one I can't stress enough.

00:55:50.751 --> 00:55:54.471
If your app returns all the fields of
a record, and you rely on the UI to

00:55:54.471 --> 00:55:58.481
filter out the sensitive stuff, then
all an attacker needs to do is go

00:55:58.481 --> 00:56:02.441
around the UI and call the API directly
and get the raw, unfiltered data.

00:56:04.021 --> 00:56:06.821
DO take time to carefully
define API standards.

00:56:07.161 --> 00:56:09.161
This falls under the governance umbrella.

00:56:09.491 --> 00:56:12.441
It's really important to have
a well defined, thought out set

00:56:12.441 --> 00:56:14.151
of API standards and policies.

00:56:14.591 --> 00:56:18.751
This will aid your entire API lifecycle,
including security and compliance.

00:56:19.911 --> 00:56:22.531
DO pressure test your
APIs for logic flaws.

00:56:22.836 --> 00:56:26.906
These are nearly impossible to detect
in real time, so make sure you test

00:56:26.906 --> 00:56:30.486
your APIs in ways they're not meant
to be used to find these flaws.

00:56:31.209 --> 00:56:34.359
DO use runtime protection
to block well known attacks.

00:56:34.889 --> 00:56:37.989
This is mandated in PCI
and is a best practice.

00:56:38.149 --> 00:56:42.429
So use a web application firewall
or other device to block volumetric

00:56:42.519 --> 00:56:44.439
attacks, injections, and others.

00:56:45.239 --> 00:56:47.869
But DON'T expect the firewall
to prevent all threats.

00:56:48.099 --> 00:56:51.679
Some are going to get through, so make
sure you're finding those vulnerabilities.

00:56:52.184 --> 00:56:55.804
especially logic vulnerabilities
through testing before production.

00:56:56.684 --> 00:57:01.794
And finally, DO train your API teams
on the OWASP API Security Top 10.

00:57:02.384 --> 00:57:06.574
This is the industry standard on API risks
for a good reason, and you definitely want

00:57:06.574 --> 00:57:08.384
your developers to understand the threats.

00:57:12.479 --> 00:57:15.569
That wraps up the API Security
for PCI Compliance course.

00:57:15.599 --> 00:57:17.789
Congratulations on making
it all the way through.

00:57:18.309 --> 00:57:19.569
I hope you enjoyed the course.

00:57:19.629 --> 00:57:22.449
